data.content:
The **lightest-weight approach** for .NET 8+ is to use the built-in [`TimeProvider`](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.timeprovider) abstract class — no DI container needed, no new NuGet packages:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In production code nothing changes (the default is real time):

```csharp
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(); // uses wall-clock
```

In tests you control time with the built-in fake:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing; // from TimeProvider.Testing NuGet

var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 12, 31, ...)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-5)); // rewind if needed
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**

| Concern | Answer |
|---------|--------|
| New interface? | No — `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL |
| DI container? | Not required — optional ctor param |
| Breaking change? | No — default keeps existing callers working |
| Test fake? | `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (single small package) |

